From 47418cd02c289fd2f376f56a7b0f5d7614a14552 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 20:20:37 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 13/26] fix: polygonal bokeh visible for the first time since
 the CE3 era

Three stacked causes kept the aperture-shaped bokeh invisible for a
decade; all fixed:

1. ToUnitDisk multiplied Shirley's signed radius back into the
   coordinate, point-mirroring half the taps while evaluating the
   polygon at the un-mirrored angle - every odd-sided polygon was
   averaged with its own mirror image back into a circle. Harmless only
   for the hardcoded centro-symmetric 8-gon it always ran with. The
   sign is now folded into the angle before the polygon lookup.
2. The circle-to-polygon blend window must overlap LARGE bokeh discs or
   the shape is never readable: round at f/1.2, fully formed by f/2.8
   (one stop past wide open, like a real iris).
3. The polygon's physical radius modulation (19% for a pentagon, 8% for
   an octagon) drowns in the quarter-res gather and TAA. New cvar
   r_DofBokehShapeStrength (default 2) exaggerates the modulation;
   lens-character material for the preset layer later.

Diagnostics that cracked it, kept in: r_DepthOfFieldBokehQuality N
forces an N-blade kernel at full blend from the console, and the values
actually reaching the kernel are logged once per change.

Also: Dof_User_BlurAmount unclamped from 1.0 to 4.0 - long lenses wide
open now produce genuinely larger discs (engine side never had a cap).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
